data srv3_host_lan_endpoint_note: String = "srv3's HOST OS endpoint, absent from this topology until 2026-07-24 — only srv3_bmc_endpoint (192.168.1.192) was modeled, so the model could name srv3's out-of-band controller but not the machine itself. Observed live from the operator's DHCP table (192.168.1.221, MAC 48:21:0B:81:C7:1D) and confirmed addressable by probe (sshd answers on :22). REACHABLE via the srv2 hop (corrected 2026-07-25): ubuntu@srv3 runs commands today authenticated by srv2's local node-orch key (proven live — hostname=srv3, aarch64, 128 cores). What is NOT yet present is the FLEET identity: the fleet-automation-ssh-key is authorized for no user on srv3 (born Jul 13 with ssh.authorized-keys EMPTY, hand-keyed post-install with node-orch@srv2 / briansrls@srv1 / macbook only), the B2 runner-user + A1 secret-materialization gap in gunbc.plans.fleet_subsumption_manual_gaps — a one-time host_authorized_keys_reconcile upsert over the working node-orch hop, not a networking fact and not an unreachable host. LEASE STATUS: as of 2026-07-24 the operator converted this to a STATIC reservation (192.168.1.221 ↔ MAC 48:21:0B:81:C7:1D), so the literal is now a fact rather than a lease-lifetime observation — matching the srv1/srv2 hosts and the srv1/srv2/srv3 BMCs. STILL DYNAMIC and therefore provisional: srv4's host (192.168.1.196) and srv4's BMC (192.168.1.195); those two literals remain only as stable as their leases until the operator reserves them. The DHCP-reservation act itself is an unmodeled manual step for srv1..4 — a static-lease intent (MAC ↔ address binding on the operator's router) has no carrier for those hosts; see gunbc.plans.fleet_subsumption_manual_gaps for where that gap is tracked. PARTIAL CLOSURE (SPARK-LANE-A, 2026-08-07): gunbc.spark.dgx_procurement's DgxSparkRouterBinding is that carrier for srv5/srv6, holding the cited extdeps.router.verizon_cr1000a reservation row Optionally and refusing deployment admission while it is Absent — and the two reservations the operator authored for the DGX Spark units ARE modeled there as observed router rows, held apart from the slot bindings until the operator states which box serves which slot. It is deliberately NOT retrofitted to srv1..4 in the same change: those four hosts are already reserved and enrolled, so a binding row for them would be authored from the live router rather than ahead of arrival, and that is a read-transport question (the cr1000a lease-table transport is itself an open extdeps frontier row) rather than the pre-arrival intent question srv5/srv6 pose."

data srv3_host_lan_endpoint: NetworkEndpoint = NetworkEndpoint {
  label: "srv3-host-lan",
  host: Present { value: operator_host_srv3 },
  address: "192.168.1.221",
  role: HostOperatingSystem,
  locality: Lan,
}

